title: Your first platformer
intro: A hero that runs and jumps, ground to stand on, a coin to grab and a slime to dodge, in a few minutes and with no code.
summary: Build a small platformer from an empty project: a hero, a level, a coin that counts a score and a slime that hurts.
project: Empty | My Platformer

chapter: A hero | Entity > Characters > Platformer Player
say: Start a new project from the Empty template (File, New Project). It opens on a scene with just a camera. The Entity menu makes ready-made things, and the first one a platformer needs is its hero.
deselect
create: Platformer Player
expectcomponents: Player = Transform, Sprite, BoxCollider, Body, PlatformerMovement
say: The hero is an orange box that already runs, jumps and falls: a Body gives it gravity and a collision shape, and Platformer Movement reads the arrow keys, WASD and Space. Its Inspector tunes how it feels, which you will try in a moment.
shot: hero

chapter: Ground to stand on | A sprite with a collider
deselect
create: Sprite
rename: Ground
set: Transform.Position = 0, -3, 0
set: Sprite.Size = 16, 1
set: Sprite.Tint = #484B55
add: BoxCollider
set: BoxCollider.Size = 16, 1
say: A level is made of boxes. A Sprite draws one, and a Box Collider makes it solid. Without a Body it never moves, so it is the floor.
expectcomponents: Ground = Transform, Sprite, BoxCollider
deselect
create: Sprite
rename: Ledge
set: Transform.Position = 4, -0.8, 0
set: Sprite.Size = 4, 0.5
set: Sprite.Tint = #6B707D
add: BoxCollider
set: BoxCollider.Size = 4, 0.5
set: BoxCollider.One Way = true
say: A second box makes a ledge. One Way lets the hero jump up through it and land on top.
shot: level

chapter: Try it | F5
play
wait: 0.8
hold: Right 0.6
tap: Space
wait: 0.8
say: Press F5 to play. Run with the arrow keys or A and D, and jump with Space. The game plays in the Game tab, and Shift+F5 stops it and puts everything back.
stop

chapter: Make it feel right | Tuning the jump
select: Player
set: PlatformerMovement.Jump Height = 3.2
say: How the hero moves is a handful of numbers in the Inspector. Jump Height is how high a jump goes, in world units. Try 3.2 and play again: change a number, press F5, feel it.
expectvalue: Player.PlatformerMovement.Jump Height = 3.2
shot: tuning

chapter: A coin and a score | Pickup and Counter
deselect
create: Sprite
rename: Coin
set: Transform.Position = 4, -1.8, 0
set: Sprite.Size = 0.5, 0.5
set: Sprite.Tint = #E3B341
add: CircleCollider
set: CircleCollider.Is Trigger = true
add: Pickup
set: Pickup.Layers = Player
say: A coin is a sprite with a trigger collider, which detects touches without blocking, and a Pickup. Pickup collects it when something on the Player layer touches it, and sends a Score message.
deselect
create: Text
rename: Score
set: Transform.Position = -6, 4, 0
set: Text.Value = Score: 0
add: Counter
say: A Text shows words in the world, and a Counter adds up Score messages into it. That is a whole scoring system without a line of code.
expectcomponents: Score = Transform, Text, Counter
shot: coin

chapter: A slime to dodge | Patrol and Damage On Touch
select: Player
add: Health
say: Enemies hurt, so the hero needs Health. Add Component finds what you type.
deselect
create: Sprite
rename: Slime
set: Transform.Position = -4, -2.1, 0
set: Sprite.Size = 0.8, 0.8
set: Sprite.Tint = #57AB5A
add: Patrol
add: DamageOnTouch
set: DamageOnTouch.Layers = Player
expectcomponents: Slime = Transform, Sprite, BoxCollider, Body, Patrol, DamageOnTouch
say: Patrol walks back and forth and brings the collider and body it needs. Damage On Touch hurts anything on the Player layer it meets.
shot: slime

chapter: Play the finished level | Collect the coin
play
wait: 0.8
hold: Right 1
tap: Space
wait: 1
expectvalue: Score.Text.Value = Score: 1
say: Run right along the ground and the coin is yours: the score counts up.
shot: finished | game | 960
stop
say: That is a platformer. Save the scene with Ctrl+S, then build it into a single exe with Game, Build Game. Next steps: draw your own sprites with the Sprite Painter, paint a tilemap level, and add sounds from the Sound Maker.
